.TH EXPR 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
expr \- evaluate an expression
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expr
.I argument ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B expr
evaluates the expression formed from its
.I argument
operands (each already one token; whatever invoked
.BR expr ,
typically a shell, is responsible for having word-split the
expression the way its grammar needs) and writes the result, followed
by a newline, to standard output.
.SS Grammar
In order of increasing precedence, every binary operator below is
left-associative:
.PP
.RS
.nf
expr1 | expr2
expr1 & expr2
expr1 { = > >= < <= != } expr2
expr1 { + \- } expr2
expr1 { * / % } expr2
expr1 : expr2
( expr1 )
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB expr1 " | " expr2
.I expr1 ,
if it is neither the empty string nor (when it is a numeric candidate;
see below) zero; otherwise
.IR expr2 .
.TP
.IB expr1 " & " expr2
.I expr1 ,
if neither
.I expr1
nor
.I expr2
is null or zero (see below); otherwise
.BR 0 .
.TP
.IB expr1\ op\ expr2
where
.I op
is one of
.BR = ", " > ", " >= ", " < ", " <= ", or " != .
.B 1
if the comparison holds,
.B 0
if not; see Numeric vs. string operands below for which kind of
comparison is used.
.TP
.IB expr1\ op\ expr2
where
.I op
is
.B +
or
.BR \- .
The integer sum or difference; both operands must be numeric
candidates, or the whole expression is invalid.
.TP
.IB expr1\ op\ expr2
where
.I op
is
.BR * ", " / ", or " % .
The integer product, quotient, or remainder; both operands must be
numeric candidates; division or remainder by zero is invalid.
.TP
.IB expr1 " : " expr2
Match:
.I expr2
is always a basic regular expression, regardless of whether it looks
numeric, implicitly anchored to the start of
.I expr1
(a match that does not start at the very first character does not
count). The result is the text captured by the first
.BI \e( \|.\|.\|.\e)
subexpression in
.I expr2
if it contains one, or, if it does not, the number of characters
matched. If
.I expr2
contains no
.BI \e( \|.\|.\|.\e)
and does not match at all, the result is
.BR 0 ;
if it does contain one but that subexpression did not itself
participate in the match, the result is the empty string.
.TP
.RI "( " expr1 " )"
.I expr1 ,
evaluated first.
.SS Numeric vs. string operands
An operand consisting only of an optional leading
.B \-
followed by one or more digits is a numeric candidate.
.BR + ", " \- ", " * ", " / ", and " %
always require both operands to be numeric candidates. The comparison
operators
.RB ( = ", " > ", " >= ", " < ", " <= ", " != )
compare as integers only when both operands are numeric candidates;
otherwise they compare as byte strings, since this library recognizes
only the C locale and has no stronger collation to fall back on.
.B |
and
.B &
(and
.BR expr 's
own exit-status decision) treat an operand as "null or zero" when it
is either the empty string or a numeric candidate whose value is 0; a
non-numeric, non-empty string is never null-or-zero.
.B :
always treats both of its operands as plain strings, regardless of
whether either looks numeric.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I argument
One token of the expression; see Grammar above. At least one is
required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The expression evaluated to neither the empty string nor a numeric
zero.
.TP
.B 1
The expression evaluated to the empty string, or to a numeric
candidate whose value is 0.
.TP
.B 2
The expression was invalid: a syntax error, a missing or extra
operand, a non-numeric operand given to an arithmetic operator,
division or remainder by zero, or an invalid regular expression given
to
.BR : .
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
This implementation has no case that exits above 2; every failure it
can detect is classified as "the expression is invalid".
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sed (1),
.BR grep (1)
